Why: every downstream change to the wave5 encoder's V4L2 layer. Kept as one patch
per upstream file, so a kernel bump that touches this file fails loudly once
instead of cascading through several patches that each edit the previous one's
additions.

(1) finish_encode error path. Upstream returns without v4l2_m2m_job_finish,
leaking the m2m job; the next STREAMOFF then deadlocks in v4l2_m2m_cancel_job
(D-state with the device mutex held, VPU unusable until power cycle). Log the
firmware error and finish the job. enc_output_info is zero-initialized because
this branch reads error_reason out of it after the getter has failed, and the
getter does not guarantee it was written on every failing return; zero is not
WAVE5_SYSERR_VLC_BUF_FULL, so an unwritten struct classifies as ERROR rather
than as a spurious recoverable failure.

(2) Result-error buffer ownership. The error path returned the OUTPUT buffer with
v4l2_m2m_src_buf_remove(), but start_encode() has already removed that source from
the ready queue after a successful ENC_PIC queue, so the buffer could stay ACTIVE
forever. Measured symptom: after a single WAVE5_SYSERR_VLC_BUF_FULL every
dma-heap input slot of the air-unit feeder stayed held and the instance produced
nothing further for the rest of its life. Track the one source buffer the firmware
owns and return it from the vb2 queue directly. A pure VLC_BUF_FULL consumes the
input frame, so complete OUTPUT as DONE. There is no valid access unit to hand
downstream, and CAPTURE must not be completed as a zero-length DONE or ERROR
buffer: GStreamer's v4l2 encoder path reads a zero-sized M2M CAPTURE buffer from
its own pool as end-of-stream. Keep the CAPTURE buffer on the m2m ready queue
instead. start_encode() only peeks that buffer, so after the firmware reports
the failed picture done, the same buffer can be reused for the next ENC_PIC.
Other firmware result failures still error both sides.

(3) Coded (bitstream) sizeimage clamp, mirroring the decoder clamp (patch 0270).
GStreamer requests a worst-case ~17 MiB coded buffer at 1080p and the CAPTURE vb2
pool is drawn from the same fixed pool as the codec's working buffers, so the
request must be refused. The ceiling is the uncompressed frame (w * h * 3 / 2)
rather than a fixed byte count: a fixed 1 MiB follows from an average frame, and
an average does not bound the frame after a total content change, which is how
VLC_BUF_FULL above was provoked. A coded frame larger than the raw frame is
pathological, so the geometry bounds the worst case without assuming anything
about content. Applied in try_fmt_cap and again in s_fmt_out, which recomputes
dst_fmt afterwards and is the value the CAPTURE pool actually allocates from; the
two must not diverge.

(4) OUTPUT plane data_offset. The encoder programmed each source plane address as
the bare vb2_dma_contig_plane_dma_addr and ignored the queued plane's data_offset,
which V4L2 defines as the offset of the plane data within the buffer. A DMABUF
importer that queues several planes of one shared allocation sets it per plane,
and dropping it makes every plane fetch from offset 0, so the encoder reads luma
bytes as chroma. MMAP buffers carry 0, so the stock path is unchanged.

(5) Source stride wider than the picture. The encoder recomputed bytesperline from
the width and discarded the caller's, so a frame whose rows carry padding could
only be encoded after a repack, which is the whole cost zero-copy import exists to
avoid. The CVISP capture node on this SoC writes 1920 pixels on a 2048-byte
stride. The hardware takes the source stride as its own field and programs the
width separately, so a wider stride is already supported and was simply never
exposed. A caller leaving bytesperline at zero gets the computed value unchanged.

(6) Coded height. The coded picture was sized from the source height after source
alignment, so padding rows landed in the stream. The two sides differ:
W5_ENC_RAW_STEP_HEIGHT is 16, W5_ENC_CODEC_STEP_HEIGHT is 8. Encoding 1920x1080
gave a correct 1088-row source geometry and then a 1088-row coded picture, whose
last eight rows are whatever the frame writer left in the tail of its allocation,
shown by every decoder as a strip along the bottom edge. Take the caller's
requested picture size for the coded side and let the codec's own frmsize
constraints round it. The source geometry is untouched.

(7) live rate control. V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_BITRATE, V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_VBV_SIZE
and VIDIOC_S_PARM mark the change pending instead of only storing it, and
start_encode applies the pending mask before queueing the next picture. Upstream
stores these and acts on them only at the next seq init, so a change on a running
instance is silently ignored.

(8) V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_FORCE_KEY_FRAME, which upstream does not implement for
this encoder. The stream carries one IDR at session start and P-frames after it,
so a receiver that joins later cannot decode and no amount of scene change
repairs it: the encoder picks intra-vs-inter against its own reference, which is
correct, so it never learns the receiver's is not. The control arms one picture
at intra_period 1 and restores the configured value on the next. The saved period
is captured only when no restore is outstanding: a second force arriving before
the restore frame runs re-enters the arming branch, and saving there would capture
the armed 1 instead of the configured period, so the restore would write 1 back
and every later frame would be an IDR for the life of the instance. Two forces on
consecutive frames are enough to latch it, which is reachable whenever a receiver
requests IDRs faster than one frame apart.

(9) Vendor parity for the open parameters that have no V4L2 control. wave5's
sequence-init writes several fields the driver hardcodes; the vendor MPP service
drives the same firmware from its own default template, which is recovered field
by field in userspace/docs/air-video-benchmark.md. Six differ, and each is set to
the vendor's value: hvs_max_delta_qp 10 -> 4, rdo_skip 1 -> 0,
lambda_scaling_enable 1 -> 0, rc_weight_buf 128 -> 1, tmvp_enable 0 -> 1 and
max_num_merge 0 -> 2. hvs_max_delta_qp is the one that bounds an intra picture:
with CU-level rate control on, it caps how far a block may be coded below the
picture QP, and 10 permits an intra picture six QP steps finer than the vendor
allows. max_num_merge 0 is not a vendor difference so much as a gap - the field
takes 1 or 2 and nothing ever wrote it. The fields the vendor agrees with
upstream on are left alone.

(10) Job timeout, .job_abort and fault counters. A completion that never arrives
leaves the m2m job TRANS_RUNNING forever; v4l2_m2m_cancel_job() then waits
uninterruptibly with no timeout while holding the mutex both video nodes are
registered with, so teardown parks in D state and the whole VPU is unusable until
a power cycle. device_run now arms a 500 ms delayed work when it leaves a job
active, the completion disarms it, and its expiry returns the buffers as errors
and calls v4l2_m2m_job_finish. The 500 ms default sits above the firmware's own ~125 ms
watchdog, whose report arrives as an ordinary completion the error path already
handles, so the timeout only covers the no-answer case. The work runs on a
workqueue taking irq_lock and never dev_lock, because the task it releases is
holding dev_lock. .job_abort moves the instance to STOP so no further job starts
while teardown waits. The firmware-error warning is latched per instance and
counted instead of printed per occurrence: it fires on one in three abandoned
teardowns, and a per-occurrence warning is a log flood rather than a signal. The
counts are reported once, at release.
The timeout is a module param, and setting it to zero restores the unbounded wait:
with inject_lost_enc_irq that reproduces the deadlock on demand, which is what
shows the recovery is the thing preventing it.

(11) STREAMOFF drain budget. The drain loop gets a total budget,
enc_drain_timeout_ms, default 2000 ms. Upstream waits VPU_ENC_TIMEOUT, 60 s, on
each pass and bounds the number of passes at nothing, so a firmware that stopped
answering parks STREAMOFF uninterruptibly: 124 s measured across two passes with
the job timeout above already armed, making this a second unbounded wait
independent of the one that timeout covers. The budget bounds both the per-pass
wait and the pass count. Zero restores the upstream behaviour.

(12) H.264 GOP. GOP_SIZE lands in avc_idr_period, and the seq-init path forwards
it into the firmware's intra_period for HEVC and not for H.264. intra_period is
the period the firmware refreshes on, which is why the forced-keyframe path in
(8) drives that field rather than avc_idr_period, and only H264_I_PERIOD writes
it. So a client setting GOP_SIZE alone got one IDR when the instance opened and
none after: measured on a DVR recording, 853 access units carrying a single IDR
where 14 were asked for. Anything joining mid-stream then has nothing to start
decoding on, which surfaced as an RTSP client being answered 503 after the
server's 20 s preroll timeout on every connection except the one racing the
encoder's creation. The H.264 branch carries the same intra_period fallback the
HEVC branch already has, so the standard control does what it says on both.

(13) profile, level and frame_rate are reported at seq init. profile and level
reach the SPS, so a stream whose SPS disagrees with them says the controls never
arrived rather than that the firmware ignored them. frame_rate is what the rate
controller divides the bitrate by and only S_PARM writes it, so a client that
never issues one leaves it at the 30 set in open(): at 1080p60, targets of 3, 4
and 6 Mbps measured 6.15, 7.42 and 10.78 on the wire, while at 720p30 a 4 Mbps
target measured 4.40. The control defaults themselves are deliberately left
upstream's - Baseline, CAVLC and level 1.0 are a policy a client is meant to
override, not a driver defect, and this line is what shows whether it managed to.

